<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amar Singh of Patiala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amar Singh was raja of Patiala from 1765 to 1782, grandson and successor of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-singh">Ala Singh</a>. He received the title Raja-i-Rajgan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> and enlarged the state through campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, making Patiala the leading Phulkian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amar-singh-of-patiala.jpg" alt="Amar Singh of Patiala" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_painting_of_Raja_Amar_Singh_of_Patiala,_ca.1830.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amar Singh was born on 7 June 1748, the son of Sardul Singh and grandson of Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-singh">Ala Singh</a>, the founder and first ruler of Patiala. He belonged to the Phulkian dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rulers, and through his grandfather claimed descent from Rawal Jaisal Singh, founder of the Kingdom of Jaisalmer in the 12th century. Early in his life <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> administered the Amrit Sanskar initiation rite of Sikhism to him.</p><p>All three of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-singh">Ala Singh</a>'s sons died before him, so on his death in 1765 the 18-year-old Amar Singh was installed on the throne by Fateh Kaur, Ala Singh's widow. His older half-brother Himat Singh revolted and seized Bhawanigarh Fort, until Fateh Kaur persuaded the two to a compromise: Amar Singh's accession was recognised and Himat Singh kept his possessions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>'s last invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1767, Amar Singh met him in person and received the title Raja-e-Rajgan Bahadur. He also paid the ransom of captives taken from the Punjab, which earned him the name Bandi Chhor, releaser of captives. His campaigns took Kotla and Issru in 1766, Tibba from the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> in 1767 and most of the land around Bathinda in 1771. In 1774 he fought the Bhattis in Hisar with heavy losses, and in 1779 he joined other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rulers in forcing a Mughal army marching into the Punjab to retreat.</p><p>He kept his grandfather's policy of good relations with both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>, and was accepted as a member of the Dal Khalsa. When he again supported the Durrani Empire against the Dal Khalsa in 1781, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassa-singh-ahluwalia">Jassa Singh Ahluwalia</a> invaded his territory and forced him to make peace at Khanna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Historians attribute his early death to the effects of alcoholism. His six-year-old son Sahib Singh succeeded him, and his daughter Sahib Kaur at times served as prime minister of Patiala and as a general in its army. He left Patiala the strongest state between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. Because his successors are generally judged frivolous, historians have called his early death a tragedy for Patiala and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and some have speculated that a longer reign could have made Patiala a rival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s Sikh Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amar Singh of Patiala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He survived by balancing between the Sikh misls and Ahmad Shah Durrani, from whom his grandson <b>Amar Singh</b> received the title Raja-i-Rajgan.</blockquote><a href="/state/patiala-state">Patiala State</a></li></ul></section>
